{
  "course": "empirical-macroeconomics",
  "title": "Empirical Macroeconomics",
  "description": "Sixty original constructed questions for an advanced undergraduate methods bridge and graduate empirical-macro course. Prerequisites: intermediate macroeconomics, econometrics, linear algebra, probability and conditional expectations, regression inference, time-series dependence and basic VAR/state-space notation. Graduate extensions include external-instrument and sign identification, forecast-error decompositions, simultaneous inference and model-based counterfactuals. The bank focuses on macroeconomic measurement and research decisions rather than repeating advanced-econometrics theorem exercises or closed-economy macro derivations. Every numerical scenario is hypothetical, and historical-policy questions assert no invented empirical findings. Official course and research materials guide coverage, not copied questions.",
